hi there steve here i'm going to do a  second uh video right here uh in seville  uh and i want to talk about language  learning and the brain because i've been  doing a lot of reading on the subject  and i kind of like reading  about the subject i read about it in  english but i also  have read about it in french in german  and portuguese and spanish because  it's an opportunity for me to read about  something that interests me in different  languages  um  i must say that  after a while i find the information  quite contradictory  it seems that every six  all of these books they seem to refer to  some of the same tests with monkeys and  rats and people and  people who had amnesia or  whatever his name was phineas somebody  or other who had a  big spike driven through his head and  and all these different things and they  draw somewhat different conclusions and  they're somewhat different  um  you know research  experiments and so forth  uh it used to be thought that the  language skills were controlled on in  the left side of the brain now the  feeling is that it's controlled on both  sides of the brain  uh some of the people say that we are do  some parts of our language in the broca  area and in some other area and  you know it's kind of contradictory so  you know it's a bit like this whole  business about whether or not eggs are  good for you and whether they have good  cholesterol or bad cholesterol i always  ate eggs never worried about it i just  felt that eggs are healthy  and i think when it comes to trying to  understand  how we learn and how the brain affects  how we learn  you know i think we all have to kind of  draw our own conclusions  and so i'm going to share with you what  some of my conclusions are and this is  not tremendously scientific other than  the fact that i've sifted through a lot  of  books on the subject  i do believe that the brain naturally  learns it learns on its own and it takes  time learning and it learns all the time  and the more stimulus you give it the  better  uh  i was quite interested in a book by a  person's name i can't remember but who  talked about what he called the the  wisdom paradox i think was the name of  the book  and his thing was that the left side of  the brain is where we have  anything that is an established pattern  and that's what we rely on  for most of our learning and most of our  reactions and that the right side of the  brain is where  new ideas new patterns are introduced  now whether in fact it's the right side  the left side or some other combination  of stuff happening the brain doesn't  matter to me but i believe that that's  true that that we establish certain  routines  and and in doing so that becomes  efficient for the brain because we no  longer have to consider all possible  combinations we just  refer to the pattern so we've excluded  say in the case of language  sounds that we didn't hear as a child  grammatical structures that we didn't  hear as a child these are now excluded  we have started to there might have been  a period when we were young  you know in our first few years where  any  pattern any language pattern chinese  french spanish whatever swahili could  have been imprinted on our brain as the  standard reliable  pattern  and so during that period it's open  season but once we  sort of settle on  one pattern then that's the pattern so  now when you want to introduce another  language it's got to come through that  innovative side of the brain  front back left right i don't care  and so i believe that's the case and  then as we get  um enough comfort with this new pattern  it stocks in the place let's call it on  the left side of the brain and now we  rely on that so now we're able to do  things more you know  unself-consciously that's one thing i  believe i also believe  that the right side of the brain  or the innovative side of the brain  is always looking in order for these  things to become a pattern  is looking for associations comparisons  to things that are already in place in  the brain  and therefore  learning  words and meaning and phrases  uh is a lot easier than learning  a theoretical structure  because for example when i go to study  russian the theoretical explanation of  russian  uh with all the rules and and exceptions  so i i i can't deal with it i've never i  have nothing to refer to but if you tell  me that in russian the word for horse is  this and the word for cow is that  i can understand that and if you say  uh  you know that people say  what is your name in this way and it's  not translated as what is your name name  uh  then okay that equates to that so i can  understand that so that learning based  on meaning to my mind is more effective  and i i mean we see it all the time uh  people with with with very large  vocabularies in english still get the  third person singular wrong it's not a  difficult concept it's the only  uh form of the verb that changes we put  an s on there he goes i go hugo i go  tomorrow whatever he went we went we  went none of those change but the third  person singular changes  simple  all kinds of people can't do it because  you've got to change a pattern in that  left side or whatever side of the brain  it is that controls these comfortable  patterns that you have so meaning  is going to be the way to bring the  language in i believe  i also believe that we need to be  attentive and we need to help our brain  to notice  and i have noticed that myself that all  kinds of things fly by me that i don't  notice until i get them wrong two or  three times or i just happen to be  listening and i hear something oh yeah  that's what they do and so then maybe  you notice it a few more times and  gradually then it moves from the  novelty innovative side to the  you know  pattern side so  you know in all of this about how the  brain learns those are the things that i  think are important we can we the other  thing i do believe is that we learn both  when we are consciously learning but  also when we are not consciously  learning and i've had this a number of  times like in my russian learning  i all of a sudden i have all these texts  unlinked that are just covered  in in yellow highlighted words those are  words that i once looked up i know them  all  i haven't necessarily flash carded them  all but i know them all  and there's a bunch of words that i'm  adding to my new word total that i never  even looked up  so that we are learning stuff  almost implicitly  and i have also observed that if i stop  my russian for a couple of months or a  month when i go back to it i've actually  improved so there's a gestation that  goes on in the brain  so the brain does a lot of learning on  its own but on the other hand the brain  is not like  an animal that you domesticate and tame  and you know you give them a little  cookie and the the dog gives you its  hand  yeah the brain likes to be rewarded it  likes to get that sense of  satisfaction but it won't act on command  and it won't learn things on command and  you can't deliberately study the  declension table and then expect that  you're going to know it you won't so the  brain  does respond to reward it does respond  to a sense of achievement the brain  likes new things stimulates it  but it doesn't necessarily act on  command  the other thing that i think is very  important about the brain and language  learning in particular is that  as we get older we of course we rely  more and more on the sort of established  patterns we have and this is something  that  in this book on the paradox of wisdom  and the wisdom paradox is pointed out  that that we become more efficient we  have this wisdom we have such a  tremendous range of accumulated patterns  that we can more easily make decisions  we know how to deal with situations  because we've seen them before  so that's a positive side on the  negative side we may become less open to  new ideas and new languages and  therefore i think that it's very good  for the brain and it tends to keep the  brain younger  and rejuvenate the brain to learn  another language i think it's a  tremendously  uh positive thing to do to stimulate the  brain in this way and we know that the  brain remains plastic throughout our  lives even though  the brain does go through certain  changes presumably so learning another  language to keep that innovative side of  your brain if we follow the  the wisdom paradox example to keep that  going stimulating forcing you to bring  new  patterns  into the sort of pattern side of your  brain keeps the brain young  so  that's my take and all of this very  often  contradictory information  and i like having my view of how my  brain works  i don't know how my car works i don't  know how the cholesterol in eggs affects  my cholesterol but i drive my car and i  eat eggs and i learn languages  based on this very rough and  unscientific superficial  summary of my reading to date on the  subject i am expected to get roundly  criticized by people much more  knowledgeable than i am on the subject  thank you for listening

